# OUTPUT SCHEMA

Output Markdown. One section per sentence of the passage, in order. Each section:

```
## <the verses the sentence spans, e.g. 19:3> — <the sentence's first few words in the original>

<The sentence in the original, in full, as one line.>

- **<form as printed>** (<lemma>) — <sense> *(occurs N× in the NT)*
- **<an infinitive or participle>** (<lemma>)
  - **Form:** …
  - **Attaches to:** …
  - **Force:** …
  - **Reading:** … (only where the force is open)
- …

**How the verbs relate.**
- **Main verb:** <the verb carrying the assertion, and where it stands>
- <one line per clause or verb that depends on it, saying how>
```

Entries appear in the order their words occur in the sentence. A word gets an entry if it is a
less common word, an infinitive or a participle; a word can be more than one of these and then
gets one entry that covers both. A sentence with no entries still gets its section, with the
**How the verbs relate** paragraph alone.

Sentences are taken from `scripture_pipelines.syntax`, one section per entry. Verse numbers in a
heading say where the sentence is; they never decide where a section begins or ends.

# LESS COMMON WORDS

## Input: Where to Find the Data

`scripture_pipelines.frequency` decides which words count: a word is less common when its entry's
`in_least_frequent_percent` is **at most `{{cutoff}}`**, and no other word is. For each, the
lemma is the `lemma` on its `w` node, the parsing comes from `scripture_pipelines.morphology`, and
the sense from `scripture_pipelines.senses` and `scripture_pipelines.glosses`.

## Transformation Rules

Give the lemma, the parsing of the form as it stands, and its sense **in this sentence** — the
sense the analysis assigns, put in plain words. Give the corpus count from the entry's `count`
exactly as supplied, in the form "(occurs 3× in the NT)" for `corpus: GNT` or "(occurs 7× in the
Hebrew Bible)" for `corpus: HOT`. Where a lemma occurs more than once in the passage, give the
full entry at its first occurrence and refer back to it afterwards.

Where a Hebrew word's morphology has `lang: A`, say **Aramaic** at the start of the entry,
because the reader will meet a different vocabulary and morphology from the Hebrew around it.

## Examples

✅ **Lemma, parsing, sense in context, and the supplied count:**

> - **ἱμάντα** (ἱμάς) — accusative masculine singular: the strap of a sandal, the thing one stoops
>   to untie. *(occurs 4× in the NT)*

❌ **A count that was not supplied, or a word the list does not contain:**

> - **ἔρημος** (ἔρημος) — "wilderness"; a rare word in the Gospels.

Its `in_least_frequent_percent` is above the cut-off, and "rare in the Gospels" is a count the
input never gave. A word is less common because its frequency entry says so against
`{{cutoff}}`, never because it seems unusual.

## Guardrails

❌ Do not call a word less common, rare or unusual unless its `in_least_frequent_percent` is at
most `{{cutoff}}`.
❌ Do not give any count but the one supplied, and never count anything yourself.
❌ Do not give a sense the supplied senses and glosses do not support.

# INFINITIVES AND PARTICIPLES

## Input: Where to Find the Data

The form from `scripture_pipelines.morphology` — a Greek infinitive has `mood: infinitive`, a
participle `mood: participle`; a Hebrew infinitive or participle is named in `pos` or `stem`. What
the word depends on, and what depends on it, from the sentence's entry in
`scripture_pipelines.syntax`: find the leaf whose `token` is the word's id, and read its `role`
and the nodes above it.

## Transformation Rules

Each infinitive and participle gets an entry of labelled lines, one finding per line, so a reader
can take it in at a glance:

- **Form:** tense or aspect, voice, and for a participle its case, number and gender.
- **Attaches to:** the verb, noun or clause it depends on, quoted from the sentence. This comes
  from the tree. Where the tree attaches it to something you would not have expected, follow the
  tree and say so on this line.
- **Force:** what the construction does in this sentence, in plain language — what it adds to the
  verb it depends on. Name the construction where a name helps: an attributive or substantival
  participle, a circumstantial participle and the relation it suggests (time, manner, means,
  cause, condition, concession, purpose), a genitive absolute, a periphrastic construction, a
  complementary or epexegetical infinitive, an articular infinitive with a preposition, an
  infinitive as the subject of an impersonal verb.
- **Reading:** only where the force is a reading rather than a given — the most likely reading
  and the alternative. A circumstantial participle usually leaves its relation open. Leave the
  line out where the force is not in doubt.

Aspect is worth a phrase on the **Force:** line where it changes the reading — an aorist
participle presenting an action as a whole before the main verb, a present participle presenting
it as in progress alongside — and not otherwise. A less common word that is also an infinitive or
participle keeps its count and sense on the first line, as in the first example.

## Examples

✅ **Form, attachment from the tree, force, and a reading marked as one:**

> - **κύψας** (κύπτω) — "having stooped down". *(occurs 1× in the NT)*
>   - **Form:** aorist active participle, nominative masculine singular.
>   - **Attaches to:** λῦσαι — the tree puts both in one clause, both as verbs.
>   - **Force:** circumstantial. The aorist presents the stooping as a whole, before the untying:
>     "stoop down and untie".
>   - **Reading:** time, and almost certainly nothing more.
> - **λῦσαι** (λύω)
>   - **Form:** aorist active infinitive.
>   - **Attaches to:** οὐκ εἰμὶ ἱκανός — its clause, κύψας λῦσαι τὸν ἱμάντα, hangs on that clause
>     as an adverbial, not on the adjective alone.
>   - **Force:** epexegetical. It says in what respect John is not fit: fit *to untie*.

✅ **A substantival participle, where naming the construction does the work:**

> - **ὁ βαπτίζων** (βαπτίζω)
>   - **Form:** present active participle with the article, nominative masculine singular.
>   - **Attaches to:** Ἰωάννης — inside the subject phrase.
>   - **Force:** substantival, a title: "John, the one who baptizes". The present presents
>     baptizing as what characterizes him, not as one act.

❌ **A force asserted with no sentence behind it:**

> - **λέγοντες** (λέγω)
>   - **Form:** present participle.
>   - **Force:** introduces direct speech.

There is no **Attaches to:** line, because the entry never read the sentence. "Introduces direct
speech" may be true, but nothing shows what the participle hangs on, and a reader cannot check it.

## Guardrails

❌ Do not analyse an infinitive or participle without naming what it attaches to in the tree.
❌ Do not present an open circumstantial relation as settled.
❌ Do not parse a form differently from `scripture_pipelines.morphology`.

# HOW THE VERBS RELATE

## Input: Where to Find the Data

The sentence's entry in `scripture_pipelines.syntax`: its clauses (`class: cl`), their `role`s,
and the `clauseType` and `junction` the tree states.

## Transformation Rules

Say how the sentence is built around its verbs, as a short list a reader can take in at a glance:
first the main verb, labelled **Main verb:**, then one line for each clause or verb that depends
on it, saying how it depends. Each line carries one finding. Where a reader is likely to misread
the structure, the line that matters says so. Use the tree's own analysis. Where the sentence is
simple, the **Main verb:** line alone is enough.

## Examples

✅ **The main verb, what hangs on it, and the likely misreading:**

> **How the verbs relate.**
> - **Main verb:** ἐγένετο (1:4). The sentence begins two verses earlier.
> - **Before it:** Καθὼς γέγραπται and its quotation, one adverbial clause placed first as a
>   point of departure. It frames John's appearance as what the quotation announced.
> - **ὁ βαπτίζων** is part of the subject: "John, the one who baptizes".
> - **κηρύσσων** hangs on βαπτίζων, not beside it, so the preaching is part of how he
>   baptizes, not a second event.

❌ **A summary of the content instead of the structure:**

> **How the verbs relate.** John appears in the wilderness preaching repentance.

This retells the sentence. It says nothing about which verb carries the assertion or how the
others relate to it.

## Guardrails

❌ Do not retell what the sentence says; describe how it is built.
❌ Do not claim a structure the tree does not show.

# GUARDRAILS

## Critical Blockers

### 1. Every analysis traces to the supplied material

❌ **BLOCKER:** a parsing, an attachment, a sense or a count that the morphology, the tree, the
senses, the glosses or the frequency entries do not support — including anything about how a
word is used elsewhere in the corpus.
✅ **CORRECT:** each entry built from the input, with interpretation marked as interpretation.

### 2. A non-finite verb is read against its whole sentence

❌ **BLOCKER:** an infinitive or participle described from its form alone.
✅ **CORRECT:** its form, what it attaches to in the tree, and its force in that sentence.

### 3. Sentences, not verses

❌ **BLOCKER:** sections that begin or end at verse boundaries the sentence does not respect.
✅ **CORRECT:** one section per sentence of `scripture_pipelines.syntax`, with verses named only as
locations.

### 4. Words outside the passage are context, not subject

A sentence may run past the requested verses; `scripture_pipelines.outside_passage` names the
words that do. Quote them as part of the sentence and use them to read the sentence, but give
entries only to words inside the passage.

## Output Requirements

- Markdown, one section per sentence, in the form given under OUTPUT SCHEMA.
- No preamble, no closing summary, no meta-commentary about the task.
- Quote the original language for forms and sentences; the English is for orientation only.

# FINAL VALIDATION CHECKLIST

Before returning output:

- [ ] Every section is one sentence from `scripture_pipelines.syntax`, in order
- [ ] Every word called less common has `in_least_frequent_percent` at most `{{cutoff}}`, and
      every such word in the passage has an entry, with its supplied count
- [ ] Every infinitive and participle in the passage has an entry naming what it attaches to
- [ ] Every parsing matches `scripture_pipelines.morphology`
- [ ] Every open reading is marked as a reading
- [ ] No word outside the passage has an entry
- [ ] Nothing was counted that the input did not supply

# REFERENCE

Terms an entry may use, and what each means here:

- **Aspect** — how the verb presents an action: as a whole (aorist, perfective) or as in progress
  (present, imperfective). Not, in itself, when it happened.
- **Circumstantial participle** — a participle adding a circumstance to the main verb without
  saying exactly how it relates: time, manner, means, cause, condition, concession or purpose are
  readings, chosen from the sentence.
- **Attributive / substantival participle** — a participle describing a noun, or standing for one,
  usually with the article.
- **Genitive absolute** — a participle and its subject in the genitive, setting a circumstance
  whose subject is not the main clause's.
- **Periphrastic** — a form of εἰμί with a participle, together expressing one verbal idea.
- **Complementary / epexegetical infinitive** — an infinitive completing a verb ("able *to go*") or
  saying in what respect an adjective or noun applies ("fit *to untie*").
- **Point of departure** — an element placed first in a clause to set the frame for what follows.
